==English==

===Etymology===
[[File:US Navy 101029-N-8848T-409 Gunnery Sgt. Robert Stahl encourages officer candidate Jared Good, from Towanda, Pa., during the first week of the 12-we.jpg|thumb|A [[drill instructor]] bawls at an officer candidate]]
From {{inh|en|enm|baulen}}, from {{der|en|non|baula||to low}} and/or {{der|en|ML.|baulō||to bark}}, both from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bau-||to roar}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bau-||to bark}}, conflated with {{cog|gem-pro|*bellaną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*ballijaną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*buljaną||to shout, low, roar}}, from {{cog|ine-pro|*bʰel-||to sound, roar}}. Cognate with {{cog|fo|belja||to low}}, {{cog|is|baula||to moo, low}}, {{cog|sv|böla||to bellow, low}}. More at {{m|en|bell}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|[bɔːɫ]}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/bɔl/}}
* {{a|cot-caught}} {{IPA|en|/bɑl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-bawl.wav|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔːl|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|ball}}<!--perhaps not in all dialects but in Australian English and in New York City-->

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[shout]] or [[utter]] in a loud and [[intense]] manner.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[wail]]; to give out a [[blare#Verb|blaring]] [[cry]].
#* '''1859''', {{w|George Meredith}}, ''The Ordeal of Richard Feverel'', Chapter 5:
#*: Why did you '''bawl''' out just as I was aiming? Who can aim with a fellow '''bawling''' in his ear? I've lost the birds through it.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[weep]] profusely. 

